\section{Elaboration Makes the Circuit Finite}

A hardware description language can describe a circuit, a simulation environment, or both. Register-transfer level (RTL) names a level of description, not a file extension: it specifies what state is stored, when it changes, and how combinational logic computes the next state. A synthesizable VHDL architecture and a synthesizable SystemVerilog module can describe the same RTL. A testbench with explicit time delays can be legal HDL without describing any circuit the synthesis tool can build.

Elaboration resolves the top-level design, binds instances, evaluates parameters or generics, expands constant-bound generate constructs, and determines widths and types. A generate loop creates multiple instances once; a clocked counter changes state every time its clock arrives. Similarly, a bounded loop in a combinational process can describe many copies of logic, not a series of clock cycles. To perform one iteration per clock, the RTL must express the iteration state, or a higher-level compiler must schedule it and emit that state.

Kasprzyk and colleagues' datapath-generation paper proposes a data-flow-controlled SSA variant for hardware generation from C. It makes data dependencies and predicates available together, for instance when deciding which definition supplies an operand. That is upstream of ordinary RTL synthesis: a compiler must still decide where values live across clock edges. The Vienna Architecture Description Language makes a related division at a different level: its ISA and microarchitecture descriptions can drive both cycle-accurate simulation and synthesizable HDL generation. The emitted HDL is an input to a further hardware flow, not evidence that an implementation has been physically realized.

