\section{Trace a Clocked Assignment into a Feedback Path}

This is IEEE SystemVerilog \cite{ieee1800systemverilog}; the fragment is also within the subset of the Yosys Verilog frontend selected by \texttt{read\_verilog -sv}.

\begin{verbatim}
module acc #(parameter integer W = 4) (
    input  logic clk, rst, en,
    input  logic [W-1:0] x,
    output logic [W-1:0] q
);
    always_ff @(posedge clk)
        if (rst)     q <= '0;
        else if (en) q <= q + x;
endmodule
\end{verbatim}

The following lowering pseudocode is a semantic model, not an HDL frontend implementation:

\begin{verbatim}
bind W to the instance parameter       # require W >= 1
old = register_output(q)              # read pre-edge state
sum = truncate_W(old + x)             # overflow is discarded
held_or_sum = mux(en, sum, old)        # no assignment means hold
d = mux(rst, zero_W, held_or_sum)      # reset wins over enable
connect_register(q, d, rising(clk))   # commit once at the edge
\end{verbatim}

With \(W=4\), \(q=13\), \(x=6\) and \(\texttt{en}=1\), the next value is \(19\bmod16=3\), unless \texttt{rst} is asserted, in which case it is zero. With neither control asserted it remains \(13\). The nonblocking assignment reads the previous state; it does not feed the sum back repeatedly during the same edge. An enable without an assignment is intentional here. The same omission in a process meant to be combinational can require a latch, because the output still has to remember something.

\Cref{fig:visual-313} isolates the two choices ahead of the register. A later pass may absorb them into the register representation, but the priority is observable at the interface.

