\section{Inspect the Intermediate Artifact}

With the example saved as \texttt{acc.sv}, these are Yosys script commands:

\begin{verbatim}
read_verilog -sv acc.sv
hierarchy -check -top acc
proc
opt
stat
write_rtlil acc.rtlil
\end{verbatim}

The \href{https://yosyshq.readthedocs.io/projects/yosys/en/latest/using_yosys/synthesis/proc.html}{Yosys documentation on process conversion} distinguishes extraction of multiplexers and flip-flops from later optimization. Here the output is RTLIL with connectivity and word-level cells, not a target netlist. Inspect the cell parameters for width, reset polarity and priority: the resulting register may have explicit reset and enable ports rather than two separate mux cells. The important test is what survives at the pins, not whether the normalized graph matches the drawing node for node.

Different boundaries answer different questions. In VHDL-2008 \cite{ieee10762008}, the \href{https://ghdl.github.io/ghdl/using/InvokingGHDL.html}{GHDL invocation guide} distinguishes analysis, elaboration and execution; for a supplied testbench named \texttt{tb}, \verb|ghdl -a --std=08 tb.vhd| and \verb|ghdl -r --std=08 tb| analyze and simulate, not map logic to a device. In synthesis, technology mapping chooses real primitives or standard cells. Placement and routing come after that choice; a logical netlist has no routed delay or pin assignment by itself. No default optimization pass can decide to retain a fifth carry bit or insert another externally visible cycle without changing this design's contract. Diagnose the width and edge behavior before arguing about how fast a particular implementation can run.
